with just one year to go  anticipation is building across the  district as stadium waimakariri starts  to take shape  the mia and councillors toured the site  last week alongside staff from the north  canterbury sport and recreation trust  who will be responsible for managing the  facility  once construction is complete  mayor dan gordon says it was wonderful  to be able to walk through the building  and get a sense of what the finnish  stadium will be like  it's really good to have come through  and have a sneak preview of  where this project's at as we can see a  lot of people have been talking about it  as they've  gone past on cold stream road and seen  the framing go up  it's looking fantastic i understand it's  well on track  and the contractors are certainly making  a good job of it as we can see  it's going to be a terrific asset to our  community this facility for  ports with all the other services that  will be here including a  gymnasium as well so it's good to see  progress and  good to see the contractors are doing  such a good job on behalf of our  community  the 28 million multi-use sports facility  will be approximately 6 000 square  meters in size  and house four indoor courts as well as  a central fitness facility  changing rooms coaching and meeting  spaces and seating for up to 500  spectators  designed by warren and mani architects  it aims to become a vibrant focus point  for sport in the district  the stadium can also easily be expanded  in the future if required  the site on cold stream road places it  near other sporting grounds  such as the main power oval main power  hockey turf  and maria andrews park  despite a small delay due to the covert  19 lockdown  construction is on schedule it's  expected that the stadium's doors will  open to the public towards the end of  july  [Music]  2021  you
 

